l priced a C$1.1B bond \"comfortably\", so investors are paying for risk rather than shutting the market. (Source: doc `utility-credit`.)\n- **Equity is much weaker than debt.** Two utility share offerings were cut back in size. Pellham withdrew its C$2.4B equity offering because demand fell short. (Sources: `utility-credit`, `pellham-offering`.)\n- **Funding needs are rising.** 2027 capex is planned up 18% at Pellham and 12% at Norvane, mostly for substations and transformers. (Source: `utility-capex-2027`.)\n- **Pellham is the stress point.** After the failed equity raise it will cut 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and defer two substation programmes \"until financing improves\". That suggests debt alone isn't filling its gap, and it may be limited by leverage or ratings. That is my inference, because the corpus has no Pellham leverage or ratings data.\n\n**What I could not find**\n- 2027 debt maturities and refinancing walls.\n- Expected 2027 issuance volume.\n- Credit ratings, leverage headroom, and the depth of buyers beyond the single Norvane deal.\n\n**Bottom line:** Debt is the open channel. Norvane-type issuers can likely fund 2027 at wider spreads, with the market sized around C$1B deals. Pellham, the largest equipment buyer, is constrained, so the risk sits in its capex and procurement. Depth beyond roughly C$1B per deal is unverified.","ttft_ms":1588,"type":"result","duration_ms":16664,"uuid":"fdeeb8be-ca6c-4b5e-a8e2-07265d4a9f1f","ttft_stream_ms":1308,"time_to_request_ms":164,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":9,"system_prompt":19,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":42,"input_hooks":7,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":7,"tool_pool_refresh":0,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":16,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":7,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":0,"autocompact":5,"request_announcements":11,"query_setup":1,"model_gates":2,"tool_schema_build":13,"message_normalization":2,"client_creation":7,"request_build":5},"t